module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_precondition_check_sits_downstream_of_the_irreversible_effect" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a check that would have refused, placed after the effect it was protecting, is not a weak wall -- it is a wall built on the wrong side of the door, and the ordering claim in the prose beside it is what stops anyone looking** (INVALID STATE: a flow declares that every precondition is decided before its irreversible operation, and one of those preconditions is in fact an effect performed AFTER it. Each step is individually correct and the sequence type-checks; only the ORDER is wrong. HARM: the protected operation is non-idempotent and its product is returned exactly once, so the refusal arrives after the only copy of the value has been created, and the failure mode is not a failed run but an orphaned live credential -- a thing that exists, grants access, and which nothing in the tree knows the identity of.",

    "RECEIPT, MEASURED ON THIS REPOSITORY: gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run carries the annotation 'EVERY PRECONDITION IS DECIDED BEFORE THE CREATE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION', and enumerates the ones it decides -- an allocated account id, a pinned bootstrap custody version, an admitted mint shape. The CUSTODY CONTAINER was not among them. store_minted_token called gcp.SecretManager.CreateSecret AFTER cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create had already minted the token, so a run lacking permission to create the container would mint a live R2 credential and then fail to store it. The mint's own refusal text has always said the right thing -- 'THE TOKEN WAS MINTED BUT ITS CUSTODY CONTAINER WAS REFUSED, so the value is unrecoverable and the token must be revoked' -- which is the tell: the flow had ALREADY NAMED this outcome as a disaster and had placed the check that prevents it downstream anyway.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVED ITS ONE EXECUTION, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A CLASS. gunbc#10947 ran this flow to completion and stored the read mint. It succeeded because the identity executing it was an operator gcloud session holding project-wide secretmanager.secrets.create, so CreateSecret returned 200 and the misordering was never observable. A GREEN EXECUTION UNDER THE MOST PRIVILEGED AVAILABLE IDENTITY IS THE WEAKEST EVIDENCE AN ORDERING CLAIM CAN HAVE: privilege makes every downstream permission check succeed, which is exactly the condition under which a check's POSITION stops mattering. The defect became visible only when the flow was pointed at a least-privilege federated identity -- not by anyone re-reading the code.",

    "DUAL OF THE FABRICATION-DOWNSTREAM-OF-THE-CHECK CLASS, AND NAMED SEPARATELY BECAUSE THE REMEDIES ARE OPPOSITE. gunbc#10739 recorded a fabricated value placed downstream of the check that catches the real value's absence: the check is correctly positioned and something false flows past it. Here the check is correctly SHAPED and incorrectly POSITIONED: nothing is fabricated, the refusal is honest, and it arrives too late to be worth anything. One is repaired by deleting the fabrication, the other by moving the check -- and a reader who has only the first row in mind will read this code as conforming, because nothing in it is fabricating anything.",

    "THE TEMPTING REPAIR IS ITSELF AN INSTANCE, AND IT IS WHAT THE AUTHOR PROPOSED FIRST. Faced with 'the identity cannot create the container', the cheap fix is to have an operator pre-create it and teach the actuator to read a 409 ALREADY_EXISTS as converged. That leaves the irreversible effect ahead of the check and merely stops one of its consequences from being reported as an error (operator ruling, 2026-09-12: 'mapping a 409 after the Create to container-present would leave the irreversible effect ahead of the check, which is the bug you found, not a fix for it'). The real repair is that the flow stops performing the write at all: the container becomes an OBSERVED precondition -- a readonly GET decided inside the same ladder as every other precondition -- and the write path narrows to the one operation that was always meant to follow the mint.",

    "NOT THE SAME ROW AS [[enforcement_gate_positioned_after_the_effect_it_guards]], AND THE TEST THAT SEPARATES THEM IS THAT ROW'S OWN NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER. Checked deliberately, because the two read as twins and section 3 forbids a twin. There, a refusal is COMPUTED BEFORE the effect and the path fails to BRANCH on it -- the verdict is in hand, correct, and only ever read at serialization time, so the run changes the world and then reports accurately that it should not have. Here NO VERDICT EXISTS at the moment of the irreversible effect, because the operation that would produce it is sequenced after: every branch is present and every branch is correct at its own site, and gunbc.cloudflare.r2_token_mint_run did refuse honestly when CreateSecret failed -- it simply refused about a token that had already been minted. The remedies are correspondingly opposite: branch on a value you already have, versus hoist an observation you never took and delete a write.",

    "THAT DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TAXONOMIC, WHICH IS WHY THE ROWS STAY SEPARATE. The landed row's trigger is an effect-ordering reader that refuses any path reaching a declared irreversible operation WITHOUT FIRST BRANCHING ON THE GUARD NAMING IT. Run that mechanism against this defect and it passes clean: no guard named cloudflare.AccountTokens.Create, and the CreateSecret refusal that existed named CreateSecret. A capability that fully closes that class leaves this one untouched, so merging the rows would retire this one behind a wall that cannot see it. The axis those rows already share is stated in the landed row's own boundary against [[declared_refusal_unreachable_from_its_own_constructor]] -- there the arm is never reached, there it is reached too late -- and this is the third point on it: the arm is reached, on time relative to its own operation, and that operation is itself the thing sequenced too late.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: FOR EVERY NON-IDEMPOTENT OR VALUE-RETURNED-ONCE OPERATION, LIST EVERY EFFECT THAT RUNS AFTER IT AND ASK OF EACH WHETHER IT COULD HAVE RUN BEFORE. Any effect that could have is a precondition sitting in the wrong place, whatever its position in the source suggests. The sharpest form of the question is about PERMISSIONS rather than logic: an operation appearing after the irreversible one needs an authorization nobody has checked for by then, so the ordering defect and the least-privilege defect are the same defect, and the flow that runs under an over-privileged identity can hold both indefinitely without either becoming visible.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and the mitigation was a human reading the actuator's call order while deriving the IAM cells a federated identity would need -- not any executing check. CEILING: structurally guaranteed. The facts needed to decide this are all modeled -- which operations mutate (extdeps.transports.rest carries RestReadExchange against RestMutationExchange, and the service declarations carry `readonly`), and which permission each effect requires (extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager secret_manager_effect_required_permissions) -- so a flow that performs a mutation whose reachable successors include an effect requiring an unheld or unchecked authorization is derivable rather than reviewable. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a CAPABILITY: an effect-ordering analysis over a func's reachable effect sequence that refuses when a readonly effect, or an effect whose required permissions are not established by that point, is reachable AFTER a non-idempotent mutation. Moving this one check, or adding one arm to this one ladder, satisfies none of it and leaves every other mint-then-store flow in the tree exposed identically.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
